| 4c | RIGHT AFTER | the walls, on the operator's machine: Docker leaves the session's user, the harness denies raw `docker` and the host shim to a session, the pūlama runs under its own user. PLAN ONLY (lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/project/road-4l-4c #/4c-stands to #/4c-acts, 2026-11-18): four walls (W0 WSL interop off, W1 Docker leaves the session's user, W2 the pūlama under its own user, W3 root-owned harness denies) and six slices (C1 the read-only `lares home walls` instrument, C2 the pūlama's own uid, C3 the shim installed outside the checkout, C4 a seat socket on a host bind so a session reaches the house without Docker, C5 the managed denies, C6 the seat mark stays), none built. Findings, measured read-only on the operator's machine: WSL interop stands on, and `wsl.exe -u root` opens a root shell with no password, so every Linux wall waits on W0; the pūlama runs as the operator's uid; the MCP seat reaches the vessel by `docker compose exec`, so the open fork of a walled session user that cannot `docker exec` takes C4's seat socket at its default (fork F-C2 (a)); the seat mark crosses the container by name (PR #267) and the UDS door reads the peer's process branch (PR #264), each a designation, never a proof. Owed: C1 to C4 before any session moves to a seat user, then the operator's one sitting (W0, W2, W1, W3, paired with the VHD compaction's `wsl --shutdown`). Holder: Lares-Alpha (backlog-alpha #/next); rank 4l's routeless kūkulu waits on it | Until they stand the pūlama keeps out drift, never a determined session; the acts belong to the operator. |

